#!/bin/sh
#
# pre-commit: run `tools/commitgate` over the content this commit is about
# to record.
#
# It checks the rules .AGENTS/AGENTS.md states that a machine can settle
# cheaply: no em dash in any added line, gofmt on the staged bytes, a
# docstring on every Go file the commit adds, and an ent schema edit that
# ships with its regenerated code and both dialect migrations. Rules
# AGENTS.md states softly, and judgement calls a static check cannot
# settle, print as warnings and do not block. See the command's own doc
# comment (tools/commitgate/main.go) for the full list and for what it
# deliberately does not check.
#
# It reads the INDEX, never the working tree, so its answer is about the
# commit rather than about whatever happens to be open in an editor. This
# is also why it does not call tools/docs-lint, which scans the working
# tree: a commit would then fail for a violation in a file it does not
# touch. `make push-gate` runs docs-lint before every push, and `make ci`
# runs it in full.
#
# Enable once per clone with `make hooks`. Skip a single commit with
# `git commit --no-verify`, which is the right move when the rule is
# genuinely wrong about your case and the wrong move the rest of the time.
#
# Nothing here is a substitute for `make ci`. It does not build, vet, test
# or scan, and it cannot tell whether a doc comment is true or a test is
# representative, which are the rules this repository cares most about.

set -eu

# exec, so commitgate's exit status is the hook's exit status and a
# refused check aborts the commit.
exec go run ./tools/commitgate
